<h3>Steps (TrueNAS SCALE)</h3>
<ol>
    <li><b>SSH service</b>: System Settings → Services → SSH: enable it and set the TCP port (matches the port above).</li>
    <li><b>User</b>: Credentials → Users → Add: use the username above, disable password login, and pick a normal shell (e.g. /usr/bin/bash).</li>
    <li><b>SSH key</b>: paste the public key above into the user's "SSH Public Key" field in the UI.</li>
    <li><b>Run the setup script</b> from a root shell (TrueNAS: <code>sudo -i</code> in the web shell). It installs the
    command allowlist, applies the ZFS delegations and rewrites the user's authorized_keys with the forced-command line.</li>
    <li>If the script says the zfs binary is not executable for your user, paste the values it prints into the user's
    <b>"Allowed sudo commands with no password"</b> field in the UI and re-run the script.</li>
    <li><b>SSH restriction</b>: paste the <code>Match</code> block printed by the script into the SSH service's
    <b>"Auxiliary Parameters"</b> field (System Settings → Services → SSH).</li>
    <li>In Unraid, go to <b>Backup and restore</b>, add a new backup entry with type <b>Remote (generic ZFS host)</b>, and test the connection.</li>
</ol>
