<div id="bb-generic-hosts-push-to-unraid" style="display:none;">
<h3>Pushing backups from a generic host to this Unraid server</h3>
<p>
This Unraid server acts as the receiver. Incoming replication connections are strictly restricted by BuddyBackup's
command allowlist (<code>restrict_zfs</code>): the remote host can receive snapshots and inspect dataset properties,
but <b>destructive operations (such as <code>zfs destroy</code>) are strictly blocked</b> to guarantee backup immutability.
</p>

<h4>1. Enable incoming backups on Unraid</h4>
<ol>
    <li>Navigate to the <b>Buddy's Backups</b> page in BuddyBackup.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Enable</b> to <b>Yes</b>.</li>
    <li>Select a <b>Destination parent dataset</b> (e.g. <code>tank/backups/buddy</code>).</li>
    <li>Configure your desired <b>Snapshot retention</b> policy (Hourly, Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Yearly).</li>
</ol>

<h4>2. TrueNAS SCALE Setup (via GUI)</h4>
<ol>
    <li><b>SSH Connection</b>: In TrueNAS SCALE, go to <b>Credentials → Backup Credentials → SSH Connections → Add</b>:
        <ul>
            <li><b>Name</b>: <code>Unraid-BuddyBackup</code></li>
            <li><b>Host</b>: Your Unraid server IP or hostname</li>
            <li><b>Port</b>: Your Unraid SSH port (default: <code>22</code>)</li>
            <li><b>Username</b>: <code>buddybackup</code></li>
            <li><b>Authentication</b>: Generate a new keypair (or select an existing one).</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Authorize TrueNAS on Unraid</b>:
        <ul>
            <li>Copy the public key generated in TrueNAS.</li>
            <li>On this Unraid server, go to <b>Buddy's Backups</b> and paste it into <b>Buddy's SSH public key</b>. Click <b>Apply</b>.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Periodic Snapshot Task</b>: In TrueNAS SCALE, go to <b>Data Protection → Periodic Snapshot Tasks → Add</b>:
        <ul>
            <li>Select your source dataset and schedule.</li>
            <li><b>Important - Naming Schema</b>: Customize the Naming Schema to:
                <br><code>autosnap_%Y-%m-%d_%H:%M:%S_daily</code> (or <code>_hourly</code>, <code>_weekly</code>, <code>_monthly</code>)
                <br><span style="color:#996600">Note: Sanoid autopruning on Unraid relies on snapshot names starting with <code>autosnap_</code> and ending with the frequency tag. TrueNAS default <code>auto-*</code> names are not recognized by Sanoid.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Replication Task</b>: In TrueNAS SCALE, go to <b>Data Protection → Replication Tasks → Add</b>:
        <ul>
            <li><b>Source</b>: Select your local TrueNAS dataset and the periodic snapshot task.</li>
            <li><b>Destination</b>: Select the remote SSH connection and specify the target dataset under Unraid's parent dataset (e.g. <code>tank/backups/buddy/truenas-data</code>).</li>
            <li><b style="color:#cc0000;">Critical - Snapshot Retention Policy</b>: Set to <b>"None"</b>.
                <br>Do <b>NOT</b> configure TrueNAS to manage snapshot retention on the destination. TrueNAS would attempt to run <code>zfs destroy</code> on Unraid, which BuddyBackup strictly rejects to protect your backups. Unraid's Sanoid service manages snapshot retention locally.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ol>

<h4>3. Proxmox VE / Debian / Ubuntu Setup (CLI)</h4>
<ol>
    <li>Generate an SSH keypair on the remote host (as root):
        <pre>ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f /root/.ssh/id_buddybackup -N ""</pre>
    </li>
    <li>Copy the public key (<code>cat /root/.ssh/id_buddybackup.pub</code>) and paste it into Unraid's <b>Buddy's Backups → Buddy's SSH public key</b>. Click <b>Apply</b>.</li>
    <li>Send snapshots using <code>syncoid</code> or native <code>zfs send</code>:
        <pre>syncoid --no-privilege-elevation --sshkey=/root/.ssh/id_buddybackup pool/dataset buddybackup@&lt;unraid-ip&gt;:tank/backups/buddy/dataset</pre>
    </li>
    <li>Add the command to cron or a systemd timer to run automatically. Older snapshots matching the <code>autosnap_*</code> convention are automatically pruned by Sanoid on Unraid.</li>
</ol>
</div>

<hr>
<h3>Security model</h3>
<p>
BuddyBackup enforces least-privilege access using SSH forced commands and a fine-grained OpenZFS allowlist:
<ul>
    <li><b>Unraid pushing to Generic Host</b>: The remote host runs the <code>generic_host_setup.sh</code> script. Validated commands are restricted to the configured parent dataset. Received data is never mounted (<code>mountpoint=none</code>).</li>
    <li><b>Generic Host pushing to Unraid</b>: The remote host connects via Unraid's <code>restrict_zfs</code> allowlist. It is permitted to send replication streams and query snapshot/resume tokens, but <b>destructive commands like <code>zfs destroy</code> are strictly blocked</b> to prevent remote malware or misconfigurations from wiping backup data.</li>
    <li><b>SSH Restrictions</b>: All connections use dedicated keys with forced commands, non-interactive execution, no PTY allocation, and no port forwarding.</li>
    <li><b>Encryption</b>: Data is transferred as raw encrypted ZFS snapshot streams. Neither server can decrypt the other's datasets without the original encryption key.</li>
</ul>
</p>
</div>
